      "lineNbr": 769,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958315
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-02T15:22:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ll put IAccessor in a new namespace \"android.os.accessor\" to separate it from other AIDLs in \"android.os\" and make \"android.os.accessor\" public so everyone can provide their own implementation of Accessor. wdyt?",
      "parentUuid": "bd096e3c_3df2fff5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 766,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 769,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "d69fc3090e197520b4c0106dcd82b3f0babc0ed5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "67081be1_8ef282e0",
        "filename": "libs/binder/Android.bp",
        "patchSetId": 14
      },
      "lineNbr": 769,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-03T00:15:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think splitting it into a separate aidl_interface probably makes sense, but I\u0027m wondering if we can keep it unstable like IServiceManager and other interfaces here.\n\nCould we pass the connection information directly into libbinder and setup and manage the RpcServer* and RpcSession objects directly in libbinder?\n\nI have mentioned this to Devin, and we should chat soon, but one reason is that currently each RpcServer has its own RpcSession and each RpcSession has its own threadpool. We\u0027re going to need to change this so that we can combine the threadpools from different RpcServer objects and potentially merge these threadpools with the libbinder threadpool.\n\nBy managing these objects internally inside libbinder, we would:\n- avoid using libbinder_rpc_unstable (this can go away, so we can make AVF updatable or use this logic in updatable clients, which is not possible in the current implementation)\n- make it possible to combine threadpools and RpcServer instances moving forward\n- make it possible to revise IAccessor over time\n\nThe flow would be the exact same, but the logic for RPC servers would be lifted into libbinder itself.",
      "parentUuid": "616d5f75_6657dc56",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 766,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 769,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "d69fc3090e197520b4c0106dcd82b3f0babc0ed5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "178f4339_72532ac6",
        "filename": "libs/binder/Android.bp",
        "patchSetId": 14
      },
      "lineNbr": 769,
      "author": {
        "id": 1644679
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-03T15:50:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Could we pass the connection information directly into libbinder and setup and manage the RpcServer* and RpcSession objects directly in libbinder?\n\nThat requires giving every client the ability to connect to any sockets we support.\n\nRight now, we have the ability for microdroid_manager, virtmgr, etc. to remain the only processes that are allowed to set up these connections. I think that\u0027s valuable as it keeps these actions restricted and allows us to move change the transport underneath the client without having to explicitly add permissions for it to connect to the different types of sockets.\n\nI do think it would be good (necessary?) to be able to set \"global\" threadpool settings like we do with IPCThreadState. Same control would be needed with FD support. This does require special code in the clients though, so maybe we have more realistic defaults so the majority of clients don\u0027t need to explicity set them. (incoming threads 2, FD support for socket types that we know support it like UDS).",
      "parentUuid": "67081be1_8ef282e0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 766,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 769,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "d69fc3090e197520b4c0106dcd82b3f0babc0ed5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b1d8c70e_88d8c23c",
        "filename": "libs/binder/Android.bp",
        "patchSetId": 14
      },
      "lineNbr": 769,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958315
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-08T12:00:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I split it into a separate aidl_interface.\n\n\u003e but I\u0027m wondering if we can keep it unstable like IServiceManager and other interfaces here.\n\nIf we provide an `accessor_launcher` binary as planned (http://b/346674270#comment9), IAccessor does not need to be stable. It will just serve as an internal contract between the libbinder and (AVF + others relevant parties)\n\n\u003e Right now, we have the ability for microdroid_manager, virtmgr, etc. to remain the only processes that are allowed to set up these connections. I think that\u0027s valuable as it keeps these actions restricted...\n\n+1. Currently only virtmgr is allowed to connect to an listening socket in the VM and it\u0027s better to keep this enforcement.",
      "parentUuid": "67081be1_8ef282e0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 766,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 769,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "d69fc3090e197520b4c0106dcd82b3f0babc0ed5",
